        <h2>What Is a Kidney Function Test (KFT)?</h2>
        <p>A Kidney Function Test (KFT) — also called Renal Function Test (RFT) — is a group of blood tests that evaluate how well your kidneys are filtering waste from your blood. Kidneys filter about 180 liters of blood daily, removing toxins, maintaining electrolyte balance, and regulating blood pressure.</p>
        <p>KFT is commonly part of a full body checkup in India and is routinely ordered for patients with diabetes, hypertension, or a family history of kidney disease.</p>

        <h2>Key KFT Parameters Explained</h2>

        <h3>1. Serum Creatinine</h3>
        <p>Creatinine is a waste product from normal muscle metabolism. Healthy kidneys filter it out efficiently. When kidney function declines, creatinine builds up in the blood.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Serum Creatinine Range:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Men: <strong>0.7 – 1.3 mg/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Women: <strong>0.6 – 1.1 mg/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Children: <strong>0.3 – 0.7 mg/dL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>High Creatinine:</strong> May indicate impaired kidney filtration. However, creatinine can also be temporarily elevated after intense exercise, high-protein meals, or dehydration. A single high reading requires repeat testing and clinical correlation.</p>
        <p><strong>Low Creatinine:</strong> Usually not clinically significant — can occur with low muscle mass or malnutrition.</p>

        <h3>2. Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) / Urea</h3>
        <p>Urea is produced when the body breaks down protein. Like creatinine, it is filtered by the kidneys. High urea indicates the kidneys may not be clearing waste efficiently.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-primary mb-2">Normal Ranges:</p>
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Blood Urea: <strong>15 – 40 mg/dL</strong></li>
            <li>BUN (Blood Urea Nitrogen): <strong>7 – 20 mg/dL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Note:</strong> Indian labs often report "Blood Urea" while Western labs report "BUN." BUN = Blood Urea ÷ 2.14. Don't confuse the two when comparing references.</p>

        <h3>3. eGFR (Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate)</h3>
        <p>eGFR is the <strong>single most important kidney function marker</strong>. It estimates how many milliliters of blood your kidneys filter per minute. It is calculated from creatinine, age, sex, and race.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="text-sm w-full">
              <thead><tr class="border-b border-hairline"><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">eGFR (mL/min/1.73m²)</th><th class="text-left p-2 text-primary">Kidney Function Stage</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline/50">
                <tr><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-green-600">≥ 90</td><td class="p-2">Normal kidney function</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-amber-600">60 – 89</td><td class="p-2">Mildly decreased (Stage 2)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-orange-600">30 – 59</td><td class="p-2">Moderately decreased (Stage 3)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-red-600">15 – 29</td><td class="p-2">Severely decreased (Stage 4)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-2 font-semibold text-red-800">&lt; 15</td><td class="p-2">Kidney failure (Stage 5 — may need dialysis)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>4. Uric Acid</h3>
        <p>Uric acid is a waste product from purine metabolism (found in meat, seafood, and certain vegetables). High uric acid can cause gout (joint pain) and kidney stones.</p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-lg p-4 my-4">
          <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 m-0">
            <li>Men: <strong>3.5 – 7.2 mg/dL</strong></li>
            <li>Women: <strong>2.6 – 6.0 mg/dL</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>5. Electrolytes (Sodium, Potassium, Chloride)</h3>
        <p>Kidneys maintain electrolyte balance. Abnormal sodium or potassium levels can indicate kidney dysfunction.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sodium (Na+):</strong> 136 – 145 mEq/L</li>
          <li><strong>Potassium (K+):</strong> 3.5 – 5.0 mEq/L (High potassium in kidney disease can be dangerous)</li>
          <li><strong>Chloride (Cl-):</strong> 98 – 106 mEq/L</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Common Causes of Abnormal KFT in India</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Uncontrolled Diabetes:</strong> The #1 cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) in India. High blood sugar damages kidney filters over time.</li>
          <li><strong>Uncontrolled Hypertension:</strong> The #2 cause. High blood pressure damages tiny blood vessels in the kidneys.</li>
          <li><strong>Excessive Painkiller Use:</strong> Regular use of NSAIDs (ibuprofen, diclofenac) can cause kidney damage over time.</li>
          <li><strong>Dehydration:</strong> Temporary creatinine elevation — very common in Indian summers.</li>
          <li><strong>High Protein Diets:</strong> Excessive protein supplementation can stress kidneys.</li>
          <li><strong>Kidney Stones:</strong> Extremely common in North India — can cause temporary kidney function decline.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Indian Diet Tips for Kidney Health</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ <strong>Stay hydrated:</strong> Drink 2.5–3 liters of water daily (more in summers)</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Control salt:</strong> Limit to 5g/day (1 teaspoon). Avoid papad, pickles, and processed snacks in excess</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Eat seasonal fruits:</strong> Watermelon, muskmelon, cucumber are kidney-friendly</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Limit if eGFR is low:</strong> High-potassium foods (bananas, coconut water, tomatoes) and high-phosphorus foods (dairy in excess, colas)</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Avoid:</strong> Excessive protein supplements, habitual painkiller use</li>
        </ul>

